                "color": "green"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "say": "A delta plus atom joined to the ring, like nitro or carbonyl, directs meta.",
          "write": "–NO₂, –CHO, –COOH: meta"
        },
        {
          "say": "Halogens are the exception. They deactivate, but still direct ortho and para.",
          "write": "–Cl: deactivating, o/p"
        },
        {
          "say": "Para is often major, because ortho is more crowded.",
          "write": "para usually major"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "Watch out for these",
      "lines": [
        {
          "say": "Check where the nitrogen lone pair sits before you count it.",
          "write": "pyridine ✗, pyrrole ✓"
        },
        {
          "say": "Alternating double bonds aren't enough. It must be planar with four n plus two.",
          "write": "✗ COT aromatic"
        },
        {
          "say": "Bromine with iron tribromide substitutes benzene. It doesn't add.",
          "write": "✗ addition product"
        },
        {
          "say": "Plan the order of steps. The first group decides where the next one goes.",
          "write": "order of steps matters"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "Remember",
      "lines": [
        {
          "say": "Cyclic, planar, fully conjugated, four n plus two.",
          "write": "CPFC"
        },
        {
          "say": "Make E plus, attack, then lose H plus.",
          "write": "E⁺ → arenium → −H⁺"
        },
        {
          "say": "Lone pair or alkyl goes ortho para. Delta plus with a multiple bond goes meta.",
          "write": "o/p vs meta"
        },
        {
          "say": "Acylation is the safe alkylation.",
          "write": "acylate, then reduce"
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
